

\section{Introduction and Motivation}

Light Detection and Ranging (LiDAR) has become one of the most important sensing technologies for three-dimensional (3D) environment perception. By measuring the time-of-flight of laser pulses reflected from surrounding surfaces, LiDAR sensors generate dense point clouds containing accurate geometric information. Unlike passive vision systems, LiDAR operates independently of illumination conditions and provides direct metric depth measurements, making it particularly suitable for mapping, navigation, inspection, and autonomous robotics applications.

Traditionally, LiDAR has been widely used in airborne mapping, topographic reconstruction, forestry monitoring, and urban planning. The increasing availability of dense point cloud data has motivated the development of automatic semantic interpretation techniques capable of extracting meaningful information from raw geometric measurements \cite{Guo2020DeepLearningSurvey,Li2020DeepLearningLiDARReview}.

Semantic segmentation aims to assign a semantic label to every point in a point cloud, enabling the identification of structures such as walls, floors, buildings, vegetation, vehicles, or industrial equipment. This capability is essential for autonomous systems that must understand and interact with complex environments. The integration of deep learning with LiDAR processing has significantly improved segmentation performance during the last decade, transforming semantic perception into a key component of modern robotics and remote sensing systems \cite{Qi2017PointNet,Qi2017PointNetPP}.

While substantial progress has been achieved in outdoor environments, the applicability of current LiDAR semantic segmentation methods to indoor UAV operations remains uncertain. Indoor scenarios introduce additional challenges including GNSS-denied navigation, cluttered environments, repetitive structures, dynamic obstacles, and strict computational constraints. These factors significantly affect both perception and localization, making indoor UAV LiDAR semantic segmentation an active and open research area \cite{Bournez2024Indoor}.

\section{LiDAR Semantic Segmentation Approaches}

Research on LiDAR semantic segmentation has evolved from handcrafted geometric descriptors toward fully end-to-end deep learning models.

Early approaches relied on manually engineered features such as curvature, surface normals, planarity, roughness, and height information. These descriptors were combined with machine learning algorithms such as Support Vector Machines (SVM) and Random Forests to classify points into semantic categories. Although these methods were computationally efficient and interpretable, they often exhibited limited generalization capabilities and required extensive parameter tuning and feature engineering \cite{Guo2020DeepLearningSurvey,Li2020DeepLearningLiDARReview,Zhao2021TechnicalSurveyPointCloudClustering}.

The emergence of deep learning transformed the field by enabling direct feature extraction from raw point clouds. PointNet \cite{Qi2017PointNet} was the first architecture capable of directly processing unordered point sets while preserving permutation invariance. However, its limited ability to capture local geometric structures restricted segmentation performance in complex scenes. To address this limitation, PointNet++ \cite{Qi2017PointNetPP} introduced hierarchical feature learning and local neighborhood aggregation, enabling the network to model geometric structures at multiple scales and significantly improving segmentation accuracy.

Although these architectures have demonstrated remarkable performance on large-scale outdoor benchmarks, their effectiveness in UAV-based indoor scenarios remains less understood. Indoor environments impose constraints that are not fully represented in conventional datasets, including severe occlusions, navigation uncertainty, and highly cluttered geometries. Consequently, evaluating the suitability of existing segmentation approaches for indoor UAV applications has become an important research direction.

