\label{fig:pointnet_segmentation}
\end{figure}

Several methodological families have subsequently emerged:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Point-based networks:} PointNet \cite{Qi2017PointNet}, PointNet++ \cite{Qi2017PointNetPP}, and DGCNN \cite{Wang2019DGCNN} directly process raw point clouds while preserving geometric fidelity. Their main advantage lies in avoiding information loss caused by discretization, although scalability remains challenging for very large point clouds.

\item \textbf{Convolution-based methods:} KPConv \cite{Thomas2019KPConv}, ConvPoint \cite{Boulch2020ConvPoint}, and RandLA-Net \cite{Hu2020RandLANet} aggregate information from local neighborhoods through specialized convolution operators. These methods generally provide an effective balance between accuracy and computational efficiency.

\item \textbf{Projection-based methods:} Architectures such as SalsaNet and 3D-MiniNet transform 3D point clouds into structured 2D representations that can be processed efficiently using conventional convolutional neural networks \cite{Milioto2019SalsaNext}. However, geometric information may be partially lost during the projection process.

\item \textbf{Transformer-based architectures:} Point Transformer \cite{Zhao2021PointTransformer}, PTMF \cite{PTMF2025}, and 3DLST \cite{Robert20233DLST} exploit self-attention mechanisms to model long-range dependencies and global contextual relationships. These models achieve state-of-the-art performance on several benchmarks but often require substantial computational resources.

\item \textbf{Hybrid approaches:} Methods combining semantic segmentation with localization and mapping systems \cite{Suleymanoglu2022IndoorMappingLiDAR}. Such approaches are particularly attractive for autonomous UAV operation because semantic and geometric information can mutually reinforce each other.

\end{itemize}

Transformer-based models currently represent the state of the art in many benchmarks due to their ability to jointly capture local geometry and global context. However, their computational requirements often limit deployment on embedded robotic platforms \cite{Zhao2021PointTransformer,PTMF2025}.

\section{UAV-Mounted LiDAR Segmentation}

Recent advances in sensor miniaturization have enabled lightweight LiDAR systems to be integrated into Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs). Compared with conventional airborne LiDAR platforms, UAV-mounted systems offer lower operational costs, greater deployment flexibility, and significantly higher spatial resolution \cite{Varney2020DALES,Kolle2021H3D}.

UAV LiDAR has found applications in numerous domains, including:

\begin{itemize}
\item Infrastructure inspection.
\item Urban mapping.
\item Environmental monitoring.
\item Construction surveying.
\item Powerline inspection.
\item Industrial asset monitoring.
\end{itemize}

The characteristics of UAV-acquired point clouds differ significantly from those of terrestrial and airborne systems. Point density often varies considerably due to flight altitude changes, sensor orientation, and occlusions. Furthermore, aerial scenes typically contain large-scale structures and highly imbalanced semantic classes, creating additional challenges for segmentation algorithms \cite{Varney2020DALES}.

Several benchmark datasets have contributed to the development of UAV-specific segmentation approaches. DALES \cite{Varney2020DALES} contains over one billion points acquired from urban and suburban aerial environments and has become one of the most widely adopted benchmarks for aerial semantic segmentation. Similarly, the Hessigheim 3D dataset provides high-resolution UAV LiDAR point clouds with dense semantic annotations suitable for evaluating advanced deep learning methods \cite{Kolle2021H3D}.

Experimental results on DALES demonstrate a clear progression in performance across different architectural families. PointNet++ achieves approximately 68% mIoU, while KPConv exceeds 81% mIoU. More recently, transformer-based approaches such as PTMF have reached over 82% mIoU, highlighting the importance of global context modeling in large-scale aerial scenes \cite{Varney2020DALES,Thomas2019KPConv,PTMF2025}.

Despite these advances, real-time deployment on UAV platforms remains challenging due to the computational complexity of state-of-the-art networks. Efficient onboard processing therefore remains an active area of research \cite{Hu2020RandLANet,Valseca2019RealtimeLiDAR}.

\section{Indoor UAV LiDAR Semantic Segmentation}

Although outdoor LiDAR perception has received significant attention, indoor UAV LiDAR remains a relatively unexplored domain. Large infrastructures such as warehouses, industrial facilities, tunnels, logistics centers, and underground environments represent important application domains where UAV-mounted LiDAR can provide rapid inspection, mapping, and monitoring capabilities \cite{Bournez2024Indoor}.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Comparison between outdoor and indoor UAV LiDAR perception scenarios.}
\label{tab:indoor_outdoor_comparison}
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|}
\hline
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Outdoor UAV} & \textbf{Indoor UAV} \\
\hline
Localization & GNSS-assisted & GNSS-denied \\
\hline
Environment scale & Large-scale & Confined spaces \\
\hline
Occlusions & Moderate & Severe \\
\hline
Point density variation & Moderate & High \\
\hline
Dataset availability & Extensive & Limited \\
\hline
Current SOTA mIoU & $>$80\% & $\sim$70\% \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Unlike outdoor environments, indoor operations occur in GNSS-denied conditions. Consequently, localization depends entirely on onboard sensing and LiDAR-based Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM) systems \cite{Suleymanoglu2022IndoorMappingLiDAR}. Semantic perception and localization therefore become strongly interconnected tasks.

From a formal perspective, semantic segmentation consists of assigning a semantic label to every point in a LiDAR point cloud acquired by the UAV. Each point is represented by spatial coordinates $(x,y,z)$ and may include additional attributes such as intensity, reflectivity, or return information. The objective is to classify points into predefined semantic categories enabling higher-level scene understanding for navigation, inspection, and mapping tasks \cite{Bournez2024Indoor}.

\subsection{Challenges of Indoor UAV Perception}

Indoor environments introduce several challenges that distinguish them from outdoor aerial scenarios.

\subsubsection{Complex Geometry and Clutter}

Warehouses and industrial facilities frequently contain shelves, machinery, pipes, cables, storage racks, and densely packed objects. These structures generate numerous occlusions and create highly irregular point distributions. Fine geometric details become particularly important because semantic classes often differ only through subtle structural variations \cite{Armeni2016S3DIS}.

\subsubsection{GNSS-Denied Localization}

Indoor UAVs cannot rely on GPS measurements. Localization must therefore be performed through LiDAR-based SLAM algorithms that estimate the vehicle trajectory while simultaneously reconstructing the environment \cite{Suleymanoglu2022IndoorMappingLiDAR}.

Localization errors directly affect semantic segmentation performance because registration inaccuracies propagate to subsequent perception modules. This dependency motivates the development of integrated SLAM and semantic perception frameworks.

\subsubsection{Limited Training Data}

Compared with outdoor benchmarks, indoor LiDAR datasets remain scarce. Most available datasets were acquired using static terrestrial scanners rather than aerial platforms. Consequently, they do not fully capture the characteristics of UAV operation, including motion-induced noise, viewpoint variability, and flight dynamics \cite{Armeni2016S3DIS,Bournez2024Indoor}.

\subsubsection{Real-Time Constraints}

Many industrial applications require real-time onboard perception. However, state-of-the-art segmentation networks often contain millions of parameters and require high-end GPUs for inference. This creates a trade-off between segmentation accuracy and computational efficiency \cite{Hu2020RandLANet,Zhao2021PointTransformer}.

\subsection{Current Performance}

Several deep learning architectures have been evaluated on indoor benchmarks such as S3DIS \cite{Armeni2016S3DIS}. PointNet++ achieves approximately 53.5% mIoU, whereas more advanced approaches such as RandLA-Net, KPConv, and Point Transformer achieve around 70% mIoU \cite{Hu2020RandLANet,Thomas2019KPConv,Zhao2021PointTransformer}.

Although these values appear relatively high, indoor segmentation remains substantially more challenging than outdoor UAV segmentation. The combination of cluttered environments, occlusions, repetitive structures, and complex topologies significantly limits performance improvements. Moreover, S3DIS was acquired using terrestrial scanners, meaning that reported results may not directly translate to UAV-based acquisitions \cite{Armeni2016S3DIS}.

\section{Datasets and Evaluation Frameworks}

The evaluation of semantic segmentation algorithms relies heavily on benchmark datasets containing manually annotated point clouds.

\subsection{Outdoor and UAV Datasets}

Several datasets are commonly used for evaluating LiDAR segmentation methods:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{SemanticKITTI} \cite{Behley2019SemanticKITTI}: large-scale autonomous driving benchmark with point-wise semantic annotations.

\item \textbf{Toronto3D} \cite{Tan2021Toronto3D}: urban mobile LiDAR dataset containing complex streetscape scenes.

\item \textbf{DALES} \cite{Varney2020DALES}: aerial UAV LiDAR benchmark containing over one billion points.

\item \textbf{Hessigheim 3D} \cite{Kolle2021H3D}: high-resolution UAV LiDAR benchmark with dense annotations.

