\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}

Semantic segmentation performance is commonly measured using:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Overall Accuracy (OA)}: The ratio of correctly classified points to the total number of points.
    \item \textbf{Per-Class Accuracy}: Accuracy computed separately for each semantic class, useful for imbalanced datasets.
    \item \textbf{Intersection over Union (IoU)}: Measures overlap between predicted and ground-truth point labels for each class:
    \[
    IoU = \frac{TP}{TP + FP + FN}
    \]
    where \(TP\) is true positives, \(FP\) is false positives, and \(FN\) is false negatives.
    \item \textbf{F1-score}: Harmonic mean of precision and recall, often used for evaluating performance in imbalanced scenarios.
    
\end{itemize}

Among these metrics, mIoU is generally considered the most representative because it balances performance across all semantic classes and mitigates the effects of class imbalance \cite{Behley2019SemanticKITTI,Varney2020DALES}.

\section{Current Limitations and Open Challenges}

Despite substantial progress in LiDAR semantic segmentation, several limitations remain unresolved for indoor UAV applications.

\subsubsection{Limited Segmentation Performance}

State-of-the-art methods such as KPConv \cite{Thomas2019KPConv}, RandLA-Net \cite{Hu2020RandLANet}, and Point Transformer \cite{Zhao2021PointTransformer} achieve approximately 70% mIoU on the S3DIS benchmark \cite{Armeni2016S3DIS}. This performance remains significantly below that achieved on outdoor UAV benchmarks such as DALES, where transformer-based methods exceed 80% mIoU \cite{Varney2020DALES,PTMF2025}.

\subsubsection{Lack of Representative Datasets}

Existing indoor datasets are generally acquired using terrestrial scanners rather than UAV platforms. Consequently, they fail to capture motion-induced artifacts, viewpoint variability, and navigation-related challenges associated with aerial acquisition \cite{Armeni2016S3DIS,Bournez2024Indoor}.

\subsubsection{Computational Complexity}

Transformer-based architectures achieve excellent segmentation performance but require substantial computational resources. Their deployment on embedded UAV hardware remains difficult due to memory, energy, and processing constraints \cite{Zhao2021PointTransformer,PTMF2025}.

\subsubsection{Robustness to Noise and Density Variations}

Indoor UAV point clouds frequently exhibit irregular sampling density, dynamic obstacles, partial occlusions, and sensor noise. These characteristics reduce segmentation robustness and challenge both classical and deep learning approaches \cite{Hu2020RandLANet,Bournez2024Indoor}.

\subsubsection{Integration with SLAM}

Most current methods treat semantic segmentation and localization as independent problems. However, indoor UAV operation requires a tight integration between LiDAR-based SLAM and semantic perception to generate consistent semantic maps suitable for navigation and inspection tasks \cite{Suleymanoglu2022IndoorMappingLiDAR}.

