& Irregular point density, GNSS-denied localization, narrow corridors 
& Integrate SLAM with segmentation; design robust models for irregular data \\
\hline
Limited real-time deployment 
& High computational cost of SOTA deep learning models 
& Create lightweight, efficient, and real-time capable networks suitable for UAVs \\
\hline
Scarcity of high-density indoor benchmarks 
& Small-scale datasets and sparse annotations 
& Expand datasets and establish standardized evaluation protocols for indoor UAV LiDAR \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


\section{Research Gap and Motivation}

The literature review reveals that indoor UAV LiDAR semantic segmentation remains significantly less mature than outdoor UAV perception.

Three major research gaps can be identified:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Limited availability of dedicated indoor UAV LiDAR datasets \cite{Armeni2016S3DIS,Merizette2025ThreeDSES}.

\item Lack of lightweight and efficient segmentation architectures suitable for real-time onboard deployment \cite{Hu2020RandLANet,Zhao2021PointTransformer}.

\item Insufficient integration between semantic segmentation and LiDAR-based SLAM systems \cite{Suleymanoglu2022IndoorMappingLiDAR,Bournez2024Indoor}.

\end{enumerate}

These limitations motivate the development of robust and computationally efficient approaches specifically designed for indoor UAV operation. Future research should focus on improving segmentation accuracy under GNSS-denied conditions while maintaining real-time performance and compatibility with onboard localization systems.

Consequently, indoor UAV LiDAR semantic segmentation remains an open research problem with significant practical relevance for industrial inspection, warehouse automation, infrastructure monitoring, and autonomous navigation. The combination of limited datasets, challenging operating conditions, and stringent computational requirements highlights the need for specialized solutions capable of providing accurate and efficient semantic perception in GNSS-denied environments. These challenges directly motivate the research objectives and methodological choices presented in the following chapters.